  "id": "576681909",
  "subject": [
    "ANE",
    "Babylonian"
  ],
  "title": "Ekitu\u0161girzal",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/unlocated",
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/temple-2"
  ],
  "details": "<p>According to Tintir = Babylon <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/btto/Q000305\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Tablet IV</a> (line 22) and <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/btto/Q004801\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Tablet V</a> (lines 95\u201396), Ekitu\u0161girzal was located in the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/54136921\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">New City</a> district of Babylon and was the easternmost landmark of the New City district and the westernmost landmark of the adjoining <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/5066375\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Kullab</a> district. It is clear that the actual boundary between those two eastern city districts of Babylon was actually a little east of this temple of the goddess B\u0113let-Eanna.\u00a0<span>Ekitu\u0161girzal was located on the bank of the eastern canal.</span></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "The temple of the goddess B\u0113let-Eanna, Ekitu\u0161girzal (\u201cHouse, Abode of Joy\u201d), was located in the New City district of Babylon, in the eastern half of the inner city. According to the Babylonian topographical text Tintir = Babylon Tablet IV, it was one of two temples in that part of Babylon.",
  "reprPoint": [
    44.426822,
    32.542611
  ],
